  <%# == The integration, as one thing to paste (SPGD-705)
      ==========================================================================================

      This REPLACES "Connect this repository", and what it replaces is worth naming, because the
      panel that stood here was not merely thin — it pointed the wrong way. It showed
      `GET /api/v1/repository` under the heading "Endpoint" and a curl against it under "Try it".
      That request sends SpecGuard nothing. It is a connectivity check. A reader who had just
      minted a credential and followed the only instructions the product shipped ended up with a
      200 and no telemetry, and nothing on the page said the write endpoint existed at all — nor
      the client gem, nor the linter, nor the MCP bridge.

      So the panel's job is now handed off rather than expanded: the whole path lives on the public
      integration guide, which is written for a reader with no credentials and no access to this
      source, and which is checked against the endpoint by
      `spec/requests/integration_guide_spec.rb`. What stays here is the one thing that page cannot
      know — WHICH repository, and WHERE its credential is.

      == Why a prompt and not a checklist

      The audience is an AI coding agent, and the work is genuinely per-project: the guide branches
      on language and test framework, and this panel cannot know either. A checklist would have to
      pick one branch and be wrong for everybody else. A prompt hands the branching to something
      that can see the repository.

      It is deliberately SHORT and deliberately not documentation. Restating the wire contract here
      would be an unverified second copy of a page that IS verified — and the copy nobody re-reads
      is the one that goes stale. `IntegrationGuideHelper#integration_guide_agent_prompt` builds the
      text, and the same method builds the reveal panel's, so the two surfaces cannot drift.

      == What is NOT here any more

      The Connection stat and its three paragraphs of prose about 401 versus 400, about what a
      rotated-but-unused key means, and about why "never" is not "not used since rotation". The
      stat survives as a compact indicator in the page header; the essay does not survive at all.
      It was explaining a table that sits on this same page — "Rejected deliveries" — in words,
      one panel above the table itself.

      Deliberately OUTSIDE the @revealed_token gate, exactly as its predecessor was: the
      instructions have to outlive the one-shot flash, or a reader who reloads the page has no way
      to learn what the key is for. %>
  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Wire this repository up", id: "wire-up") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <% if has_api_keys %>
        <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
          Hand this to your coding agent. It names this repository, says where the key is, and sends
          the agent to the guide to work out the rest for whatever language and test framework this
          project actually uses.
        </p>

        <%# Its own `copy-text` scope. `copy_text_controller.js` reads the SINGULAR `sourceTarget`,
            so every copyable payload on a page opens its own scope or they fight over which one
            the Copy button copies. %>
        <div data-controller="copy-text">
          <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new(multiline: true) do %><%= integration_guide_agent_prompt(
                repository: @repository,
                guide_url: integration_guide_url,
                endpoint: root_url.sub(%r{/+\z}, "")
              ) %><% end %>
        </div>

        <%# The prompt names the SECRET, never a token, and this sentence is why. `ApiKey` stores a
            SHA-256 digest and the plaintext lives for exactly one flash-backed render, so this
            surface — which is every render after that one — has nothing to inline and must not
            pretend otherwise. The reveal panel's copy of this prompt does carry the real value,
            which is the one moment it can.

            The recovery half is gated at `keys_manage`, and the gate is on the POINTER rather than
            on the sentence: `#api-keys` is the id of a panel rendered only for that permission, so
            an ungated link would dangle for exactly the reader who cannot act on it. Telling a
            `view` member that the value is unrecoverable is still worth saying — it is why the
            prompt they are looking at names a secret instead. %>
        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          The prompt refers to the key by the name of the CI secret, not by its value: SpecGuard
          keeps only a digest and cannot show an existing key again.
          <% if repository_policy.can?(:keys_manage) %>
            If it has been lost, regenerate it in
            <a href="#api-keys" class="underline">API keys</a> below — the replacement is shown once,
            with the prompt already carrying it.
          <% end %>
        </p>
      <% elsif repository_policy.can?(:keys_manage) %>
        <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
          This repository has no API key yet, so there is nothing for CI to authenticate with.
          Mint a key in <a href="#api-keys" class="underline">API keys</a> below — the prompt to hand
          your coding agent is shown with the key already in it, once.
        </p>
      <% else %>
        <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
          This repository has no API key yet, so there is nothing for CI to authenticate with. Ask
          <%= @repository.user.display_name %> to mint one.
        </p>
      <% end %>

      <%# Rendered on all three branches, and that is the point of putting it outside the split: the
          guide needs neither a key nor an endpoint to be worth reading — the linter it documents
          needs neither either — so a reader who cannot mint a credential is not a reader with
          nothing to do. %>
      <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
        Reading it yourself:
        <%= link_to "the integration guide", integration_guide_path, class: "underline" %>
        covers the Ruby client gem, the intent annotation syntax, the linter that needs no
        credential at all, and the complete ingest wire contract for a project in a language that
        has no client.
      </p>
    </div>
  <% end %>
